\section*{Derandomizing Space-Bounded Computation}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 103; 2026-09-23), the asserted result is that polynomial-time randomized logarithmic space can be simulated deterministically in logarithmic space, giving $\mathrm L=\mathrm{RL}=\mathrm{BPL}$.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
For uniform polynomial-time, logarithmic-work-space Turing machines, the claimed complexity-class equality is
\[
 \mathrm L=\mathrm{RL}=\mathrm{BPL}.
\]
More quantitatively, fix any randomized machine $\mathcal M$ of polynomial worst-case time and $O(\log(n+2))$ work space. Given input $x$ of length $n$ and unary $1^q$, $q\ge1$, a deterministic algorithm is claimed to output an integer $0\le a\le2^{q+2}$ satisfying
\[
 |a2^{-(q+2)}-p_{\mathcal M}(x)|\le2^{-q}
\]
in $O(\log(n+2)+q)$ work space and $(n+2)^{O(1)}2^{O(q)}$ time. Constants depend only on the fixed machine.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 103; 2026-09-23), stochastic atoms are assembled with controlled numerical error and bounded suspended storage. Slice-mixing walks are generated without retaining their endpoints, and a fixed bounded-resource interpreter makes the deterministic simulation uniform over source machines.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 103; 2026-09-23).

\subsection{Formal verification}
No Lean formalization is listed at this snapshot for manuscript 1 (family 103; 2026-09-23).

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

